# Ermo das Águias, documentation summary

**Location and scale:** left bank of the Côa, near Vale de Madeira (Pinhel municipality), roughly 600-1000 ha depending on source. Access is a rural track descending from the village to Ribeira de Gaiteiros, where the reserve entrance sits, about 2 km from Vale de Madeira.

**History:** granite-dominated landscape with extensive rocky outcrops. Long history of cereal cultivation, olive groves, and intensive grazing, followed by recurrent fires, most recently a fire in August 2025 that burned around 300 ha of the site, spreading from a Cinco Vilas fire into neighbouring Pinhel. All animals survived, per Rewilding Portugal's own statement. Only extensive cork oak patches persisted through the fire history, thanks to cork's commercial value and fire resistance. The rest of the landscape is now dominated by white broom (*Cytisus multiflorus*), following land abandonment.

**Species:**

| Species | Notes |
|---|---|
| *Equus ferus caballus*, Sorraia breed | Semi-wild herd, now "more than two dozen" individuals, dun/grullo colouring with primitive markings, indigenous to SW Iberia, introduced 2022 |
| Tauros cattle (*Bos taurus*, back-bred type) | Herd of 15 released April 2023, cross of rustic Iberian breeds including Maronesa with aurochs-related ancestry, partner: Stichting Taurus |
| *Cytisus multiflorus* (white broom) | Now the dominant shrub across the site |
| *Quercus suber* (cork oak) | Surviving patches, the only tree cover to persist through repeated fires |
| Iberian wolf (*Canis lupus signatus*) | Site falls within the LIFE WolfLux project area, managing habitat conditions for wolf subpopulations south of the Douro, not necessarily resident, but landscape-managed for it |

One source, Wildlife Portugal's "Botanical endemisms of Ermo das Águias," specifically discusses how horse movement through the broom is opening space for greater plant diversity and dispersal, worth reading in full if you want named endemic species rather than the general description I've got here, the snippet didn't give species names.
